  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1026 AM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nUpper high centered over New Mexico with fair amount of recycled \nmoisture trapped under the high over the CWA. PWs ranging from .80 \nto 1.00 inch, with dewpoints in the 40s to 50. A few thunderstorms \nwere able to continue much of the overnight period across northern \nDona Ana County to Black Range/Grant County. Those thunderstorms/ \nshowers have mostly ended at this time but debris clouds will \npersist for a few hours. Expect slight chance of mountain \nthunderstorms again this afternoon from around noon to 6-7 pm. As \nmentioned above, debris clouds lingering over the Black Range may \ndelay thunderstorm development a few hours in that area. \n\nSunday through Wednesday...upper high nearly stationary over New \nMexico but slowly drifts toward the Arizona border. Little chance of \nthunderstorms but always a non-zero chance of an isolated \nthunderstorm over the mountains. Temperatures will remain very \nwarm/hot with high temps each ranging from around 98 to 105; \napproaching late August record high temperatures. Temperatures may \napproach Heat Advisory for the El Paso/lower valley zones.  \n\nThursday into the weekend...upper high drifts into eastern \nArizona, bringing a more northerly flow pattern over the CWA. So a\nreturn of low POPs is warranted for the mountains. Can't rule out\na lowland storm or two also, mainly over Otero, Sierra and \nnorthern Dona Ana Counties, though this isn't reflected in the \ngrids at this time.",
